# From explanation to source

The checklist example showed fragments by use. We can now describe their model
more precisely. The purpose of the model is not mathematical decoration; it lets
us reason about completeness, cycles, ordering, and the point at which generating
a file is safe.

## Projects, documents, and contributions

A Literate project is an ordered collection of Markdown documents together with
its templates and settings. A document may contain ordinary prose, diagrams, and
fragment [contributions](term:contribution). Each contribution adds source text to
a named [fragment family](term:fragment-family).

The first contribution defines a family. Later contributions may append to it.
[References](term:reference) inside those contributions name other families. A root associates one
family with an output path and programming language.

```mermaid
classDiagram
    class Project {
        ordered documents
        templates
        settings
    }
    class Document {
        prose
        contributions
    }
    class FragmentFamily {
        name
        ordered contributions
    }
    class Contribution {
        source location
        literal text and references
    }
    class Reference {
        target family
        source location
    }
    class OutputRoot {
        destination
        language
        optional template
    }

    Project "1" *-- "many" Document
    Document "1" *-- "many" Contribution
    FragmentFamily "1" o-- "many" Contribution
    Contribution "1" o-- "many" Reference
    Reference --> FragmentFamily
    OutputRoot --> FragmentFamily
```

The diagram describes concepts, not current TypeScript classes. Implementations may
change while these relationships remain the language's stable vocabulary.

## The fragment graph

A graph is a collection of nodes connected by edges. Here each fragment family is
a node and each reference is a directed edge from the family containing the
reference to the family it names.

We may write this as `G = (V, E)`, where `V` is the set of fragment families and
`E` is the set of references between them. The notation says nothing mysterious:
it is simply a compact way to name all fragments and all of their connections.

A root chooses an entry point into this graph. Tangling follows every reachable
edge and replaces each reference with the target family's ordered contributions.
Fragments that cannot be reached from that root do not appear in its output, but
the complete project must still be structurally valid.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Root[Output root] --> Main[Main program]
    Main --> Read[Read the input]
    Main --> Transform[Transform the data]
    Main --> Report[Report the result]
    Transform --> Validate[Validate one item]
```

## Definitions may follow references

The book's reading order does not need to match the direction of the graph. A
parent may name a child before the child's definition appears. This lets the author
present the whole before descending into detail.

Contribution order still matters. If three chapters append to one family, their
parts appear in deterministic project order. Forward references therefore remove
a declaration-order restriction; they do not make the project unordered.

## Expansion preserves context

When a reference occupies an indented line, its expanded text inherits that
indentation. Literal text remains literal. Escaped opening delimiters allow the
book to discuss fragment notation without creating a reference.

Expansion is recursive: a referenced family may itself contain references. A
completed expansion can be reused, but its insertion context still matters because
indentation belongs to the reference site.

Templates add one controlled outer transformation. A source template contains a
`[CODE]` marker, and Literate substitutes the complete tangled code into that
marker. This is a literal, single-pass operation rather than a general template
language.

## Invalid graphs produce no plan

Two failures are easiest to visualize. A missing reference points to no node. A
cycle leads back to a family that is already being expanded.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Start[Select an output root] --> Visit[Visit its fragment family]
    Visit --> Target{Does every reference have a target?}
    Target -->|No| Reject[Report diagnostics and produce no files]
    Target -->|Yes| Active{Is the target already on this path?}
    Active -->|Yes| Reject
    Active -->|No| Expand[Expand referenced families]
    Expand --> More{More references?}
    More -->|Yes| Target
    More -->|No| Template[Apply the optional source template]
    Template --> Plan[Add the complete output to the plan]
```

Other validation protects names, paths, templates, sizes, and contribution forms.
The common rule is more important than the individual checks: no generated file
is returned until the whole project has a valid plan.

If any diagnostic is found, the output list is empty. A host may display partial
inspection information to help the author, but it must not treat partial knowledge
as permission to replace files.

## Planning precedes effects

Tangling answers, “what bytes should every output contain?” It does not answer,
“when should the filesystem change?” That distinction keeps the semantic engine
independent of a terminal, editor, or storage provider.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Snapshot[Captured project snapshot] --> Parse[Parse and validate]
    Parse --> Graph[Build the fragment graph]
    Graph --> Outputs[Plan all outputs]
    Outputs --> Valid{Any diagnostics?}
    Valid -->|Yes| Problems[Publish problems; write nothing]
    Valid -->|No| Host[Return the complete plan to the host]
    Host --> Commit[Host preflights and commits the plan]
```

The main book will return to every stage in more depth. The next part begins with
the project snapshot, because deterministic generation depends on knowing exactly
which documents, templates, and open editor buffers belong to one interpretation
of the project.
